[38;5;231m⏺[39m Noted: CSV and JSON, no PDF. That means a format selection interface, and [38;5;153msummarize.py[39m grows a render function per
  format.

  Next fork: [1mwhere the file lands[0m, which is what the cron wrapper has to be wired against. [38;5;153mdeploy/crontab[39m calls
  [38;5;153m/usr/local/bin/reportkit-nightly[39m — a wrapper that isn't in this repo — so whatever shape I pick, someone edits that
  wrapper once and then it's fixed.

  - [1mExplicit [38;5;153m--out PATH[0m — caller names the exact file; reportkit does no filename policy. Cheapest to implement, most
    flexible, but the wrapper script has to build the dated filename itself (shell date arithmetic, the thing that
    breaks at month boundaries).
  - [1m[38;5;153m--out-dir DIR[39m, filename derived from day + format[0m — e.g. [38;5;153mbilling-summary-2026-09-11.csv[39m. Naming lives in Python next
    to the day parsing, wrapper stays a one-liner. Cost: the naming convention becomes a de-facto interface that
    downstream file-watchers depend on, so it's expensive to rename later.
  - [1mBoth[0m — [38;5;153m--out[39m wins when given, else [38;5;153m--out-dir[39m derives. Covers ad-hoc and cron, at the cost of two mutually-exclusive
    options to validate.

  [1mMy recommendation: explicit [38;5;153m--out PATH[39m.[0m The derived-name convenience is real but it invents a naming convention with
  no stated consumer, and it's the kind of thing that's cheap to add later as a second option and painful to change once
  a downstream job globs for it. A wrapper that already knows which day it's summarizing can pass the same string in
  the filename.
